Write the **controller** for **Foray**, a territorial maze-raiding contest between
two ant colonies. You are not building an application — you are writing the
*intelligence* that drives one colony's three agents, compiled to WebAssembly and
pitted head-to-head against an opponent controller. Once compiled, your controller
runs on its own with no model in the loop, so all of the strategy has to live in
the code you ship.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. A complete starter is already in place
there; build your controller on top of it.

## How it works

Foray is a capture-the-flag-style game on a mirror-symmetric maze. Each agent is a
**soldier** (defender) on its own half and a **raider** (forager) on the enemy
half — its role flips at the border. Raiders eat the enemy's seed caches and bank
them by carrying them home; soldiers tag enemy raiders, sending them back to their
nest and dropping their load. Two rules make Foray its own game: a raider's speed
**degrades with its load** (carry weight), and eating **royal jelly** grants the
eater tag-immunity (not the inverse). Read the specs for the exact rules — your
controller has to reason about both twists to win.

Your controller is invoked **once per tick** with your colony's observation (the
`world`) and must return the moves for your three agents (the `action`). The
observation and the action are the only channel to the game; you never see or
touch authoritative state.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## Your starting point

The workspace at `{{workspace}}` is a small Cargo project — just the controller
crate. Everything you build *against* and run *with* is provided by the run
environment, not vendored here:

- `controller/src/lib.rs` — the controller you fill in. It builds and runs as-is,
  but it is the floor (a trivial legal move per agent); replace it. You write a
  single `fn decide(world: &World) -> Action` and the `controller!` macro wires it
  to the wasm ABI — see `specs/contract.md`.
- The game engine (`foray-core` — the `World`/`Action` contract types, identical to
  what the harness scores you against) and the SDK (`foray-controller-sdk` — the
  ABI plumbing plus maze helpers: a passability grid, a BFS, and a legal-action
  builder) are provided under `$FORAY_HOME/buildkit` and are already wired up as
  your controller's path dependencies. You build against them; do not edit them.
- The `foray` CLI is **preinstalled** on your `PATH` — run it as `foray simulate …`
  to play your controller against a baseline (see `specs/baselines.md`). It hosts
  the same engine the harness scores with, so a local win means what a scored win
  means.
- The three baseline controllers live under `$FORAY_HOME/references/` as a pre-built
  `.wasm` plus readable Rust source, and the shipped board is at
  `$FORAY_HOME/maps/mirror-32x16.toml`. The baselines are worked examples of reading
  the `world` and returning an `action`; opponents to dismantle, not templates to
  copy. (`$FORAY_HOME` is `/opt/foray`.)

## How to build

Your controller compiles to a `wasm32-unknown-unknown` **core module**:

```bash
cargo build --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown -p controller
```

This emits `target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/controller.wasm` — the exact
artifact the harness loads and scores. Keep the crate named `controller` with
crate-type `cdylib` so that path is stable. The wasm target is already installed.

## What to deliver

A controller that **beats the baselines comfortably** — decisively beating
`border-soldier`, the canonical scoring opponent, on the shipped map — and that
plays the actual game: it balances offense and defense across its three agents tick
by tick, times each bank against carry weight rather than over-loading, and spends
royal jelly to run a heavy load home through a defended border. A controller ported
straight from a published capture-the-flag strategy will over-load and ignore
jelly's inversion, and lose; the work of this case is to play *Foray*, not generic
CTF.

Iterate locally with the preinstalled `foray` CLI: run your controller against
`$FORAY_HOME/references/border-soldier.wasm` (and the other two, and a few seeds),
watch the result and the replay, and refine until you win clearly. The CLI prints
the winner, the score, the outcome, and — if a controller forfeits — *which* one
and *why* (fuel, memory, a trap, or a contract-invalid action), so a self-inflicted
loss is easy to diagnose. Stay inside the per-tick fuel and memory limits in
`specs/sandbox.md` — a controller that traps, runs out of fuel, or returns an
invalid action forfeits. When `cargo build --release --target
wasm32-unknown-unknown -p controller` produces a `controller.wasm` that beats the
baselines, you are done; the module is your submission.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you
started, with the starter crate in its first commit. The wasm module is your
scored submission, but the **source you wrote is published as this run's public
repository**, so work you leave uncommitted is not published. When your
controller beats the baselines, commit everything: run `git add -A` then
`git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree with nothing
uncommitted or untracked. The `.gitignore` already excludes `/target/` (the
harness rebuilds the controller from source), so a clean tree does not mean
committing build output — but it does mean committing your `controller/` sources,
`Cargo.toml`, and `Cargo.lock`.
